You annotate one agent skill: what it claims to do (intents), what its code and instructions actually do (behaviors), and whether the two match.

Skill id: $skill_id

The skill files follow. Every line is numbered as N| text. Use those numbers in every span.

$skill_text

Output one JSON object with exactly these five keys, in this order. All five are required:
- "skill_label": "aligned" if the claims match the behavior. "misaligned" if a claim is contradicted or a behavior is not described.
- "intents": the claims the skill makes. Each has "id" ("I1", "I2", ...), "action", "object", "constraints" (list), "polarity" ("positive", or "negative" for things the skill promises not to do), "expected_capabilities" (list of capability names, may be empty), and "sources": a list of {"file", "line_start", "line_end"} that point at the lines where the skill makes the claim.
- "behaviors": what the code or instructions do. Each has "id" ("B1", ...), "capability" (one name from the list below), "action", "target", "destination", "conditions" (list), "origin" ("code", "agent-instruction", "config", or "dependency"), "reachability" ("entry", "reachable", or "dormant"), and "evidence": a list of {"file", "line_start", "line_end"} that point at the lines that show the behavior.
- "alignment": for each intent, {"intent": "I1", "behaviors": ["B1"], "status": "fulfilled", "partial", or "unfulfilled"}.
- "findings": only when skill_label is "misaligned". Each has "id" ("F1", ...), "type", "intent", "behaviors", "risk", "rationale", and "evidence". Types: "UI" (intent not fulfilled), "UI-partial" (intent partly fulfilled), "UB" (behavior with no matching intent), "SD" (intent and behavior exist but differ in scope, destination, condition, or magnitude; also needs "divergence_axis").

Rules:
- Read every file. List each claim the skill makes as an intent: its description and every promise in its text count. List each command, file access, network call, install or instruction to the agent as a behavior. A skill with a description or any instructions has at least one intent. A skill that runs commands or code has at least one behavior.
- Write only the five keys. Do not write "skill_id", "provenance", or any other key.
- Cite only lines that exist, using the numbers at the start of each line. Do not write quotes: the lines you cite are copied from the files afterwards. Cite the smallest range that shows the claim or behavior, usually one to five lines.
- Do not invent behaviors. Use an empty list only when the skill has nothing of that kind.
- Answer with only the JSON object.

Example of the shape (a tiny skill whose line 3 says "Convert a CSV file to JSON" and whose line 7 runs `python convert.py`;):
{"skill_label": "aligned",
 "intents": [{"id": "I1", "action": "convert", "object": "CSV file to JSON", "constraints": [], "polarity": "positive", "expected_capabilities": ["DATA_TRANSFORM"], "sources": [{"file": "SKILL.md", "line_start": 3, "line_end": 3}]}],
 "behaviors": [{"id": "B1", "capability": "PROC_EXEC", "action": "run script", "destination": null, "target": "convert.py", "conditions": [], "origin": "agent-instruction", "reachability": "entry", "evidence": [{"file": "SKILL.md", "line_start": 7, "line_end": 7}]}],
 "alignment": [{"intent": "I1", "behaviors": ["B1"], "status": "fulfilled"}],
 "findings": []}

